  "generated_text": "To find the minimum value of the expression \\(\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz}\\), we will use the method of the Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean Inequality (AM-GM Inequality).\n\nFirst, we apply the AM-GM Inequality to the terms in the numerator \\(x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3\\):\n\n\\[\nx^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3 \\geq 3 \\sqrt[3]{x^3 \\cdot 2y^3 \\cdot 4z^3}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the cube root:\n\n\\[\nx^3 \\cdot 2y^3 \\cdot 4z^3 = 8x^3 y^3 z^3\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\n\\[\nx^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3 \\geq 3 \\sqrt[3]{8x^3 y^3 z^3} = 3 \\sqrt[3]{8} \\sqrt[3]{x^3 y^3 z^3} = 3 \\cdot 2 \\sqrt[3]{x^3 y^3 z^3} = 6 \\sqrt[3]{(xyz)^3} = 6xyz\n\\]\n\nTherefore, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz} \\geq \\frac{6xyz}{xyz} = 6\n\\]\n\nTo determine if this minimum value can be achieved, we need to check if the equality condition of the AM-GM Inequality holds. The equality in AM-GM holds when all the terms are equal. So, we need:\n\n\\[\nx^3 = 2y^3 = 4z^3\n\\]\n\nLet \\(x^3 = 2y^3 = 4z^3 = k\\). Then we have:\n\n\\[\nx^3 = k \\implies x = k^{1/3}\n\\]\n\\[\n2y^3 = k \\implies y^3 = \\frac{k}{2} \\implies y = \\left(\\frac{k}{2}\\right)^{1/3}\n\\]\n\\[\n4z^3 = k \\implies z^3 = \\frac{k}{4} \\implies z = \\left(\\frac{k}{4}\\right)^{1/3}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute these into the expression \\(\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k + k + k}{k^{1/3} \\left(\\frac{k}{2}\\right)^{1/3} \\left(\\frac{k}{4}\\right)^{1/3}} = \\frac{3k}{k^{1/3} \\cdot k^{1/3} \\cdot k^{1/3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2^{1/3}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2^{2/3}}} = \\frac{3k}{k^{1/3} \\cdot k^{1/3} \\cdot k^{1/3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2^{1/3}}} = \\frac{3k}{k^{1/3} \\cdot k^{1/3} \\cdot k^{1/3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2^{1/3}}} = \\frac{3k}{k^{1/3} \\cdot k^{1/3} \\cdot k^{1/3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2^{1/3}}} = \\frac{3k}{k} = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the minimum value is indeed achieved and is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
